<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/foundational/grammar-of-cinema">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Film is a language learned, not born into — and like language it has grammar. A
    <strong>shot</strong> is a word (wide = context, close = feeling, insert = information); a
    <strong>cut</strong> is syntax (what follows changes what the previous shot meant);
    <strong>montage</strong> builds thoughts no single image contains. The founding demonstration is
    the <strong>Kuleshov effect</strong>: Lev Kuleshov's early-1920s experiment intercut the same
    expressionless face of actor Ivan Mosjoukine with a bowl of soup, a coffin, a woman — and
    audiences "saw" hunger, grief, desire in an unchanged face. The meaning was manufactured
    entirely between the shots. That experiment is film theory's Big Bang: cinema's unit of meaning
    is not the image but the <em>relationship</em> between images, which is why this lesson walks the
    grammar (how shots and cuts construct meaning), the movements that first theorised it (the 1920s
    silent schools), the film's other half (sound, movement, colour), and the contexts — social,
    political, institutional — inside which every film is made and understood.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from a Paris café in 1895 to a fully digital cinema — the grammar evolved with the machinery" widget="timeline" config={filmTimeline} />

  <h2>The core grammar, in one scene</h2>
  <p>
    Run the rules through any competent scene. <strong>Establish then compress:</strong> a wide shot
    tells you where everyone is; closer shots then trade geography for emotion — the wide is the map,
    the close-up is the weather. <strong>The 180° rule</strong> keeps the camera on one side of an
    imaginary line between characters so screen direction stays consistent and the audience never
    loses the map of who faces whom; crossing the line disorients — allowed, but breaks should
    <em>mean</em> something (a crossing at a betrayal reads as the world flipping). <strong>Match on
    action</strong> hides the cut inside a movement (hand reaches — door opens), making time seem
    continuous; <strong>jump cuts</strong> violate it on purpose to make time visible (Godard's
    <em>Breathless</em>, 1960, turned the "error" into a style). <strong>Mise-en-scène</strong> —
    everything placed in the frame: staging, lighting, costume, colour, depth — speaks before anyone
    acts: a character lit from below is a different character from one lit softly from the side,
    before a word is spoken. <strong>Camera movement</strong> is a narrator's voice: the dolly-in
    intensifies, the track accompanies, the whip-pan startles, the handheld documentary shot
    authenticates. And <strong>sound is half the film</strong>: score tells you how to feel before
    anyone speaks; a room tone that suddenly vanishes tells you more than dialogue could; the
    scream at the end of <em>Psycho</em>'s shower sequence is dubbed — Bernard Herrmann's shrieking
    violins doing what no image can. The point of the grammar is <strong>invisible authorship</strong>:
    every deliberate choice you notice on a rewatch is the director deciding you shouldn't have
    noticed it the first time.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> realism is not the default and style is not decoration. Every
  film is <em>constructed</em> — the uninterrupted long take is as much a style as rapid montage,
  just a quieter one. "Neutral" filming already encodes choices (where the camera sits, whose eye
  height, what is offscreen); there is no unstyled film, only styles you have stopped noticing.</p>

  <h2>The 1920s silent film movements: cinema theorises itself</h2>
  <p>
    Silent cinema's final decade is when filmmakers — with no sound to lean on — pushed the frame
    itself to its expressive limits, and the schools that formed are film studies' foundational
    chapters. <strong>German Expressionism</strong> (Caligari, 1920; <em>Nosferatu</em>, 1922;
    <em>Metropolis</em>, 1927) externalised psychology into design: painted shadows, distorted sets,
    acting as geometry — the anxious post-war German soul rendered in crooked architecture. Its
    visual DNA migrated to Hollywood with the émigré directors and survives in film noir and every
    horror film's lighting. <strong>French Impressionism</strong> (Delluc, Dulac, Epstein, L'Herbier)
    chased subjectivity — superimpositions, distortions, rhythmic printing — the image as interior
    state. <strong>French Surrealism</strong> offered the dream directly: Buñuel and Dalí's
    <em>Un Chien Andalou</em> (1929) opens with a razor slicing an eye — cinema declaring itself the
    art of the unconscious, indifferent to narrative. <strong>Soviet Montage</strong> was the
    theorist's school: Kuleshov's experiments and Eisenstein's <em>Battleship Potemkin</em> (1925)
    — the Odessa Steps sequence, where soldiers descend on civilians in accelerating, colliding
    shots — demonstrated that editing could construct an emotion, an argument, even a political
    thesis no single shot contains. Eisenstein classed montage from metric (cut to length) to
    tonal to <em>intellectual</em> (two images colliding to produce an idea) — editing as dialectics.
    And in counterpoint, <strong>Hollywood classicism</strong> was perfecting invisibility:
    continuity editing that serves story so smoothly the apparatus disappears. The 1920s thus
    produced the field's permanent argument: cinema as constructed, expressive art (the montage and
    expressionist line) versus cinema as faithful window on reality (the classical line) — a debate
    that outlived the silent frame itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Shot sizes and their grammar: the full scale</h2>
  <p>
    The shot-size vocabulary deserves its own map, because each step of the scale does a distinct
    narrative job. The <strong>extreme wide shot</strong> establishes scale — a figure dotting a
    desert — and makes statements about isolation or insignificance. The <strong>wide/long
    shot</strong> shows bodies whole in their environment: geography, blocking, who can see whom.
    The <strong>medium shot</strong> (waist up) is conversation's home — gesture plus face. The
    <strong>close-up</strong> claims interiority: the face alone, thought made visible, which is why
    the close-up is called the cinema's great invention over the theatre. The <strong>extreme
    close-up</strong> — an eye, a keyhole, a dial — is information or obsession. Two subtleties
    separate professionals from beginners: <strong>angle</strong> carries stance (a low angle
    empowers its subject; a high angle diminishes; a dutch tilt announces instability) and
    <strong>editing rhythm sets the clock</strong> — the average shot length of classical Hollywood
    ran many seconds, of modern action films often under two, and the same events feel frantic or
    languid purely by cut rate. A quick deconstruction exercise proves the system: take any thriller
    interrogation scene and list the shots — wide to set the room, over-the-shoulder pairs for
    conversation, a slow push-in as the question sharpens, an insert of the tell-tale object, the
    held close-up before the confession, a cutaway that withholds. Nothing in that sequence is
    natural; every element is a decision from this grammar, assembled so smoothly that first-time
    viewers only feel the tension.
  </p>

  <h2>The contexts: films do not fall from the sky</h2>
  <p>
    "The contexts of film" is the syllabus's name for the fact that every film is embedded.
    <strong>Historical and political context</strong>: <em>Battleship Potemkin</em> is Soviet
    propaganda commissioned for the revolution's twentieth anniversary; <em>Citizen Kane</em>
    (1941) is partly a cipher of newspaper baron William Randolph Hearst, who did his best to
    suppress it; New Hollywood of the 1970s is post-assassination, post-Vietnam America's cynicism
    on screen. <strong>Institutional context</strong> — the business form — shapes the art as
    decisively: the studio system's assembly line (contract players, house styles), the
    Paramount antitrust decision (1948) that broke studio ownership of theatres and helped end the
    system, the <em>Jaws</em>/<em>Star Wars</em> era (1975–77) that reorganised Hollywood around the
    wide-release blockbuster and merchandising (ILM was founded for <em>Star Wars</em>'s effects),
    and today's streaming platforms, whose release windows and algorithmic dashboards are as
    formative as any studio head. <strong>Social and cultural context</strong> includes
    representation itself: who is on screen, who is behind the camera, and how the medium's codes
    carry ideology — film studies' post-1970s core, from feminist film theory (Laura Mulvey's 1975
    "male gaze") to the ongoing audits of whose stories get financed. Reading a film "in context"
    means holding all of these at once: the shot, the grammar, the movement it belongs to, and the
    world that paid for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Meaning and response: representation and the aesthetic experience</h2>
  <p>
    Two claims sit under the whole subject. First, film is a <strong>medium of
    representation</strong>: it shows a world, and the showing is never neutral — casting, framing,
    editing and sound decide whose perspective the audience inhabits, which facts are withheld,
    which bodies are central. A documentary is as constructed as a fantasy; point of view is
    manufactured shot by shot. Second, film is an <strong>aesthetic medium</strong>: the experience
    is sensory and formal — rhythm, composition, the pleasure of a perfectly timed cut — which is
    why two films with identical plots can be entirely different works. The professional viewing
    method (Bordwell and Thompson's, in essence) runs the layers deliberately: rewatch one scene
    three times — once for story, once with sound only, once muted, counting cuts and noting where
    your eye goes; then ask what each choice <em>did</em>. Meaning and response are the same event
    seen from two sides: the filmmaker arranges stimuli (meaning), the audience undergoes them
    (response) — and the gap between the intended and the received is where interpretation lives.
    Hitchcock's famous <strong>bomb theory</strong> compresses the whole craft into one thought
    experiment: two people talk at a table and a bomb goes off — that is <em>surprise</em>, fifteen
    seconds of shock. Show the audience the bomb under the table first — plain, ticking, while the
    small talk continues — and the same scene becomes <em>suspense</em>: minutes of engagement,
    because the audience now knows something the characters do not and every innocent line about the
    weather tightens the screw. Information management — what the audience knows, when, relative to
    the characters — is the director's master lever, and it is pure grammar: a point-of-view shot, a
    withheld insert, a cutaway to the detonator, each redistributes knowledge between the viewer and
    the characters on screen.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The critical debates worth knowing by name. <strong>Realism versus formalism</strong> is the
    field's oldest axis: André Bazin (<em>What Is Cinema?</em>, essays 1940s–50s) argued cinema's
    essence is the faithful image — hence his preference for long takes, deep focus (the
    <em>Citizen Kane</em>/Welles end of the tradition, letting viewers choose what to look at) and
    ambiguity, against Eisenstein's position that meaning is <em>made</em> by cutting; the debate is
    really about where cinema's truth-claim comes from — the world photographed, or the mind
    assembling. <strong>Auteur theory</strong> is its offspring: Truffaut's 1954 essay in
    <em>Cahiers du cinéma</em> argued the director is a film's author the way a writer is a novel's,
    elevating Hitchcock and Hawks from entertainers to artists; Andrew Sarris imported it to
    America ("auteur" as critical sieve), Pauline Kael attacked it ("Circles and Squares", 1963:
    the theory credits directors for ensemble work and slights collaboration) — and the compromise
    everyone now works with is auteur-<em>ism</em>: a strong directorial signature matters, inside a
    deeply collaborative art. <strong>And the grammar itself keeps evolving:</strong> intensified
    continuity (faster average shot lengths, wider lenses, closer framing — measured across
    decades by David Bordwell), the long-take renaissance enabled by digital stabilisation
    (<em>1917</em>'s stitched "single shot"), and streaming's two-minute attention audits all push
    the grammar in real time. The timeline's arc — sensation (1895), trickery (1902), theory (1925),
    sound, colour, deep focus, cut-as-violence, blockbuster, digital — is best read as one
    sentence: cinema learned to speak, then argued forever about what saying means.
  </p>
